# Benchmarks

The benchmark suite looks at three choices you make when solving an optimization
problem: the algorithm, its implementation, and the linear-algebra backend. Each
comparison answers a different question. These guides explain the setup, walk
through the results, and describe the limits of each measurement.

## Comparing Solvers

**Which algorithm makes useful progress on my kind of problem?** The
[solver comparisons](./solvers/) run Basin's optimizers from shared starting
points and plot the objective gap against elapsed time. They show both the speed
of convergence and the effect of the starting point.

Start here when choosing between algorithms. Read the results alongside the
[solver selection guide](../docs/solvers/), which covers derivative requirements,
constraints, and problem structure.

## Comparing Libraries

**How does Basin compare with other implementations?** The
[library comparisons](./competitors/) examine individual solvers, including
L-BFGS, Nelder–Mead, Levenberg–Marquardt, and COBYLA. Each section explains what
the harness matches and where the implementations differ.

These curves measure the complete optimization path recorded by each harness.
Differences in line searches, stopping rules, and numerical safeguards can affect
both the work performed and the result.

## Choosing a Backend

**How does backend choice affect the cost of a solve as the problem grows?** The
[backend comparisons](./backends/) hold the solver and problem fixed while
varying the parameter and matrix types. They cover vector operations, dense
matrix methods, and sparse least squares.

Use these results to compare backends within a case. The cases use different
problems and stopping rules, so their solve times do not rank the algorithms.

## Reading the Results

| Comparison | What varies | What the figures show |
| ---------- | ----------- | --------------------- |
| Solvers | Basin's optimization algorithm | Objective gap against time, with a shared time budget |
| Libraries | The solver implementation | Objective gap against time, with case-specific stopping rules |
| Backends | The vector and matrix representation | Mean solve time against problem size |

In convergence figures, the **objective gap**, also called suboptimality, is
$f(\mathbf{x}) - f^\star$, where $f^\star$ is the known optimum. A smaller gap
means a better objective value. For constrained problems, check feasibility as
well: a low objective at an infeasible point does not solve the problem.

The figures use logarithmic axes where indicated. Equal distances then represent
equal ratios, and tiny positive display floors keep zero objective gaps visible.
Reaching a display floor does not establish that a solver's convergence test
passed. An endpoint can also reflect a budget limit or another stopping rule.

Each guide records its measurement date, machine, and timing procedure and
includes reproduction commands. Absolute times depend on the machine, and these
selected problems provide evidence for the measured cases. Benchmark your own
objective before making a performance-sensitive choice.

To explore how the algorithms move through a landscape, try the
[interactive visualizer](../visualizer/).
